// enrich/worker — the one blocking call, kept off the loop.
//
// Looking an address up is an HTTP round-trip: a few hundred milliseconds on
// a good day, and the full timeout on a bad one. Made on the event loop it
// would freeze the window, and freezing the window of a monitoring tool is
// how a reader concludes the machine has locked up.
//
// So the call runs on its own OS thread. `spawn` hands back a
// `Task<string>`; the loop asks `isAlive()` once a frame, which is a peek and
// not a wait, and joins only once the answer has arrived.
//
// # Why the failure is a value and not an exception
//
// Both lookups catch. A raise here would have to cross the thunk and be
// re-stashed for the `join` to re-raise, which works — but it also makes the
// `spawn` itself a throwing call, so every caller has to carry an error set
// for an exception that cannot happen until the join. The caller's job is to
// decide when to ask again; what it needs for that is "did this answer",
// which is exactly what an empty body says. A network that is down and a
// service that is angry are the same decision here, and neither is worth a
// second control-flow path.

use std::net::HttpClient;
use std::lang::IOException;

/**
 * POST `body` to `url` and answer the response body, or the empty string
 * when the request did not complete. Runs on the spawned thread.
 */
fn post(url : string, body : string) : string {
    try {
        let client : HttpClient = new HttpClient();
        return client.post(url, body).body();
    } catch e : IOException {
        return "";
    }
}

/**
 * A lookup that does nothing, answering at once.
 *
 * It exists so that a task slot is never *unassigned*. The two slots are
 * ordinary locals holding handles, and a handle that was never minted is a
 * number with no entry behind it — read at scope exit, that is a crash with
 * nothing in the program to point at. Filling both slots with a task that
 * has already finished costs two thread launches at startup and removes the
 * only state in which a slot means nothing.
 */
fn idle() : string {
    return "";
}
